<p><span style="text-decoration: line-through;"><em>Placeholder. Be right there!</em></span></p>
<p>So, another reminder that clinical tone isn't my strong suit but I am better with diaogue.</p>
<p>Alright,</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+Let's&nbsp;do&nbsp;it!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-Let's&nbsp;do&nbsp;it!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><br />
<strong><span style="font-size:1.5em;">ConProcs</span></strong></p>
<p>Clinical tone, so far, so good</p>
<blockquote>
<p>As per Veil protocols</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'Veil protocols' &lt;<span style="text-decoration: line-through;">-</span>I don't know, but I really liked this. Sounds so professional.</p>
<p>I saw no significant flaws with the clinical tone. Alright, time to hit the logs once more!</p>
<p><strong><span style="font-size:1.5em;">VIDEO LOGS</span></strong></p>
<p><strong>Containment Log</strong></p>
<p>You have a helicopter pilot called <strong>Big Brother</strong> and I freakin' love that.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Brooks: Alright, game faces on.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I really dig this.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Miller: Alright. You heard the lady. Williams, get the cobweb.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Swift, got a smidgen of attitude, but significantly subdued in the heat of a mission. Also, I like the fact that they called it a 'cobweb'.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Miller: Look at this poor thing. It looks like it's been through a lot. (pauses) I think it's still recording.</p>
<p>(Miller stares into the lens and gives a cocky grin before turning in the direction of the truck.)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Much better. A scene like this is infinitely more charming after you showed them being professional and soldier-like; doing it this way shows that they're still human, just hella trained to be a bunch of units.</p>
<p>Though, cocky grin is a little too casual. I'd suggest 'arrogant smile' or something similar.</p>
<p><strong>Log 1</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Brooks: Uh, you guys seeing this?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm still not really a fan of the 'uh', since I attribute that more to confused civilians rather than MTF troops who expect to see weird stuff. But it's only 1 compared to the boatload from before, so I guess it can slide. Maybe it's just a small initial moment of shock before it tapers off into acceptance.</p>
<p>Is is just me or did Log 1 feel shorter? Or maybe it's so much stronger, I didn't have to stop and comment on every little thing ^_^</p>
<p>Either way, not bad. I dig what I read. More professional and a it evokes a bigger sense of mystery.</p>
<p><strong>Log 2</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Brooks: This place just never ends.</p>
<p>Carter: How far have we traveled?</p>
<p>Richardson: Not sure, probably 4 clicks?</p>
<p>Carter: Feels like we've been going in circles.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I feel like removing Brooks' line and making him say 'feels like we've been going in circles' would make it stronger, but I get the feeling you're trying to make him somewhat of a tiny bit more 'complainy' than the others, but in a significantly reduced way that's not just straight up teenage whininess. Idk. Maybe I'm being too nitpicky and overanalyzing.</p>
<p>&#8230;Yeah, I'm just overanalyzing at this point.</p>
<p>Regardless, the dialogue has been significantly improved.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><em>(The herd pays no attention to the MTF. They are fleeing from something.)</em></p>
</blockquote>
<p>You can replace the period with a semi-colon ;<br />
Since the second half of the sentence is communicating something as a continuation from the first. I'm doing a very poor job at explaining it, but that's how semi-colons are used.<br />
Uh, apologies if that was a little unclear.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><em>(Another deep rumble, this time much closer. Something large and scaly moves inside the fog.)</em></p>
</blockquote>
<p>You can definitely replace 'scaly' with 'reptilian', I think.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>(Leaves rustle and a snapping sound like someone breaking twigs is heard).</p>
<p>(The camera pans upward to see a large reptilian eye staring at Brooks.)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This whole scene is so Jurassic Park-like, and I love it. I even heard the Jurassic Park theme in the back of my mind when I read this.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><em>(The Tyrannosaurus Rex bites down on Brooks, lifting his body.)</em></p>
</blockquote>
<p>'bites down on Brooks and lifts him off the ground' would work better, I'd say.</p>
<p>Overall, the more serious and professional tone of the logs is definitely a significant improvement, though I still feel like something is missing here. There's danger. There's banter. But, I think it feels like it's going by too quickly, almost. Hm.<br />
I think it's because we don't stop and explore any of these timey wimey based locales in depth. We touched base with an oldies car, tribal people and a t-rex, but then what? It almost feels like a guided tour instead of an in-depth exploration log.</p>
<p>Like, with a concept like this, I imagine the team getting chased by the T-rex, running away and ending up in the futuristic sci-fi land and sliding under a vertical closing metal door just in time for the T-rex to zap itself or something. Stuff like that?<br />
Not something as simple as Brooks becoming its lunch, Miller stumbling around in a fog for a few days, showing us a glimpse of the futuristic city and then saying, 'lmao fuk that' and leaving.</p>
<p>It's like going to Disney World or Universal and deciding that you'd rather just watch the rides and stare at the lines rather than go inside.</p>
<p><strong>Log 3:</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p><em>(An animal shrieks in the distance.)</em></p>
</blockquote>
<p>The fact that this happens but is ignored is a nice touched, I'd say. He's so used to the weird stuff that happens that he doesn't even care.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Miller: You know, I don't have a lot of regrets in life, come to think of it. I had a great childhood, married a great woman, had two precious girls. And then, for some reason, I got this job. I thought, oh hey, there's something I can get behind. A job that really makes a difference, you know? Containing threats and capturing dangerous entities. And not for fame or fortune either, since no one outside the Foundation knows about us. That we're out here, you know, protecting them. But you know what? That's fine. At least I used to go home every day proud that I was making the world a better place for my family.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is still a good monologue, if a bit&#8230;overly plainly stated? No. I think it's because I've already looked at this once, maybe now I know all the tricks this story is attempting to showcase.<br />
Either way, the buildup to this point still doesn't feel like it gives a good enough payoff.</p>
<p>Log 3 ain't bad and it works to show Miller getting more and more worn down by the timey wimey fog, but I don't feel too invested in it. Like, I'm curious about where he's going and the downward spiral he's experiencing, but not <em>super</em> into it.</p>
<p><strong>Log 4:</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>(The camera activates. Miller is staring at a stack of stone obelisks pouring out a black liquid similar to ferrofluid. They line up the road.)</p>
<p>Miller: Jesus. What are those things?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Honestly, I agree. What <em>are</em> those things?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Miller: (interference) — I trus— (interference) … and th— (interference) … (interference) … knew all along.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>For this line, I only have two words.</p>
<p>'Hell yeah.'</p>
<p><strong><span style="font-size:1.5em;">Final thoughts</span></strong></p>
<p>The ending is now significantly stronger, and the journey here is definitely a lot better, but it still feels lacking. Like this anomaly has so many time periods smushed together, but all we're doing is just marking things off the checklist.</p>
<p>Dinos? Check.</p>
<p>Cave/tribesmen? Check.</p>
<p>Giant robot in the future? Check.</p>
<p>Oldsmobile? Check.</p>
<p>It feels too&#8230;safe. This article right now feels like it's playing things way too safe.</p>
<p>We need it to be more daring. We have an anomaly where dinos and robots can exist at the same time while hippies can drive underneath the both of them and tribesmen can throw spears at said hippies and yet it never feels like it goes anywhere with this amazing concept.</p>
<p>I'm happy you removed the scene where the soldier was peeing in the woods, though.</p>
<p>'Does-business-in-woods-only-to-get-jumped-by-threat' scenes are so&#8230;lazy imo.</p>
<p>Any who, I hope this followup crit helped.<br />
Unfortunately, I will decline all future looks, since looking at the same concept and/or draft twice is my limit. Fresh eyes are very important for any piece of work.</p>
<p>I bid you good luck on the rest of your journey!</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

