<p><span style="text-decoration: line-through;">Placeholder! Got summoned via PM to take a look-see. Will crit in a bit.</span></p>
<p>Alright, here’s my take.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>To start, I should say that you write some solid, realistic dialogue. For a new SCP author, that’s a huge skill to have on your side.</p>
<p>There are, however, places where the “stage directions” of the dialogue could be clinicized a bit. I’m no expert at this, but here are a few examples:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>(The camera pans to face some sort of insect-like machine further down the road. It moves forward on twelve legs in a completely fluid manner. It resembles a strandbeest.)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Unfortunately, I don’t think “strandbeest” counts as clinical vocabulary.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>(The figures get closer; a group of tribesmen with spears approaching the road.)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think “tribesmen” refers more to early anthropologists’ descriptions of native Africans, so might want to avoid that. Still, I’ve gotten some pushback from other critters on that, so it’s up to your discretion.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>(The herd pays no attention to the MTF. They are fleeing from something.)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The “They are fleeing from something” feels more like a stage direction than an objective description of what is occurring, if that makes sense.<br />
I’m sure there are other issues, but those are the ones that stuck out to me.</p>
<p>Moving on, I’m not so sure how I feel about the logs. The stinger at the end is certainly potent, but the garbled/interfered dialogue mixed with the almost anecdotal events that Miller encounters makes it difficult to see what arc you’re trying to accomplish here. I can see that you're trying to show Miller getting more and more worn down, but the death of the random kid feels somewhat out of place / sudden, and the thrown camera seems somewhat counter-intuitive considering what he's gone through so far.</p>
<p>Oh, and also, one last nitpick: velociraptors have never been found in North America. T-rexes? Totally.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
If you respond to this comment and would like me to see it, please send me a Wikidot PM, as I will not be notified otherwise.
